\chapter{Documents and the Session in C\#}\label{ch:win-model}

This chapter is the counterpart of Chapter~\ref{ch:model}. Its three files have the same roles as
there: the document decodes a file once into a [[PixelBuffer]] and a display image; the variant,
zone and pane types describe what the window shows; and the [[Session]] holds all the state of one
window and performs the operations. Most of the logic is unchanged. Three things are not. Decoding
goes through WIC instead of ImageIO. SwiftUI's automatic observation is replaced by an explicit
change event, which is the largest difference in the whole app. And the plumbing that found the
right session for a menu shrinks, because on Windows every window has its own menus.

\section{The image document}

A macOS document was a value conforming to [[FileDocument]], created by the document group. On
Windows there is no document framework to conform to, so [[ImageDocument]] is a plain immutable
class with two factory methods, one for a file and one for a test pattern. The application object
calls them on a background thread (Chapter~\ref{ch:win-overview}). Nor is there a
[[fileWrapper]] to refuse: with no framework, there is no Save command to disable.

<<Windows/LineScope.App/Model/ImageDocument.cs>>=
using System.IO;
