@

The facts about the source are the same as on macOS. A C\# \emph{positional record} declares them in
one statement: the compiler generates the properties, a constructor and value equality.

<<Win ImageDocument: source information>>=
/// <summary>Facts about the file as decoded, before conversion to the working float buffer.</summary>
public sealed record SourceInfo(
    string FileType,
    int PixelWidth,
    int PixelHeight,
    int BitsPerComponent,
    int BitsPerPixel,
    string ColorSpaceName,
    bool HasAlpha,
    double? Dpi);
@

The document holds the float buffer, a display bitmap and the facts. As on macOS, the display image is
rendered \emph{from the buffer}, not taken from the decoder, so what the user sees is exactly what is
analyzed. The constructor is private, so a document can only come from the two factories.

<<Win ImageDocument: stored data>>=
public PixelBuffer Buffer { get; }
public BitmapSource DisplayImage { get; }
public SourceInfo Info { get; }

private ImageDocument(PixelBuffer buffer, SourceInfo info)
{
    Buffer = buffer;
    DisplayImage = Bitmaps.FromBuffer(buffer);
    Info = info;
}
@

\subsection{Decoding with WIC}

Reading a file has the same three steps as in Chapter~\ref{ch:model}: decode the first image, bring it
into the working format, and record what the file was.

<<Win ImageDocument: reading a file>>=
/// <summary>Decodes an image file. Safe to call off the UI thread.</summary>
public static ImageDocument Load(string path)
{
    <<Win ImageDocument: decoding the first frame>>

    <<Win ImageDocument: converting to the working buffer>>

    <<Win ImageDocument: recording facts about the source>>
}
@

WPF's [[BitmapDecoder]] is a thin layer over WIC, which chooses the codec from the file's contents.
Three options matter. [[PreservePixelFormat]] keeps the frame in its stored pixel format, so the
facts below describe the file and not a conversion. [[IgnoreImageCache]] makes sure the file is
really read again, not served from WPF's cache of recently opened images, which would show a stale
image after the file was changed on disk. [[OnLoad]] decodes everything immediately, so the file
stream can be closed when the method returns. A file with several frames, such as an animated GIF or
a multi-page TIFF, contributes its first, like [[CGImageSourceCreateImageAtIndex(source, 0, nil)]].
Errors are exceptions, which the caller turns into a message.

<<Win ImageDocument: decoding the first frame>>=
using var stream = File.OpenRead(path);
var decoder = BitmapDecoder.Create(
    stream, BitmapCreateOptions.PreservePixelFormat | BitmapCreateOptions.IgnoreImageCache, BitmapCacheOption.OnLoad);
if (decoder.Frames.Count == 0) throw new InvalidDataException("The file contains no image.");
BitmapFrame frame = decoder.Frames[0];
int w = frame.PixelWidth, h = frame.PixelHeight;
if (w <= 0 || h <= 0) throw new InvalidDataException("The image is empty.");
@

On macOS, [[PixelBuffer(cgImage:)]] drew the image into an 8-bit sRGB context, and Core Graphics
converted the colors from the image's embedded profile on the way (Chapter~\ref{ch:pixels}). WIC
doesn't convert colors unless asked, so the port asks explicitly: the frame is converted to sRGB and to
premultiplied BGRA, the bytes are copied out with a stride of four bytes per pixel, and the core turns
them into a float buffer ([[FromPremultipliedBgra8]], Chapter~\ref{ch:win-core}). The result is the
same sRGB-encoded, straight-alpha buffer the Mac produces.

<<Win ImageDocument: converting to the working buffer>>=
var profile = frame.ColorContexts is { Count: > 0 } contexts ? contexts[0] : null;
BitmapSource sRGB = ToSrgbPbgra32(frame, profile);
var bytes = new byte[w * h * 4];
sRGB.CopyPixels(bytes, w * 4, 0);
var buffer = PixelBuffer.FromPremultipliedBgra8(w, h, bytes);
@

Like the macOS version, the port ignores the EXIF orientation tag, for the reason given in
Chapter~\ref{ch:model}: LineScope analyzes the pixel grid as stored. WIC never applies the tag on its
own; the orientation is only metadata, and LineScope doesn't read it. Doing nothing is therefore
the whole implementation.

The facts need some work, because WIC reports them differently. The file type comes from the codec's
name: ``PNG Decoder'' becomes ``PNG image''. WIC gives the bits per pixel, and one \emph{mask} per
channel, so the bits per component are their quotient, for instance $24/3 = 8$ for [[Bgr24]]. An image
has alpha if its format has an alpha channel or, for indexed images such as GIF and 8-bit PNG, if a
palette entry is not fully opaque. The color space is named after the embedded profile, and an image
without one is taken to be sRGB, as the web and Windows assume.

<<Win ImageDocument: recording facts about the source>>=
var format = frame.Format;
int channels = Math.Max(format.Masks.Count, 1);
string codec = decoder.CodecInfo?.FriendlyName ?? "Image";
var info = new SourceInfo(
    FileType: codec.Replace(" Decoder", "", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase).Trim() + " image",
    PixelWidth: w,
    PixelHeight: h,
    BitsPerComponent: format.BitsPerPixel / channels,
    BitsPerPixel: format.BitsPerPixel,
    ColorSpaceName: profile is null ? "sRGB (no embedded profile)" : ProfileDescription(profile) ?? "Embedded ICC profile",
    HasAlpha: HasAlphaChannel(format) || (frame.Palette?.Colors.Any(c => c.A < 255) ?? false),
    Dpi: frame.DpiX > 0 ? frame.DpiX : null);
return new ImageDocument(buffer, info);
@

A pattern becomes a document exactly as on macOS, with the facts of a float buffer.

<<Win ImageDocument: a generated test pattern>>=
/// <summary>A generated test pattern, shown in the same analysis window as a file.</summary>
public static ImageDocument FromPattern(TestPatternSpec spec)
{
    var buffer = TestPatternGenerator.Make(spec);
    return new ImageDocument(buffer, new SourceInfo(
        FileType: "Generated test pattern",
        PixelWidth: buffer.Width,
        PixelHeight: buffer.Height,
        BitsPerComponent: 32,
        BitsPerPixel: 128,
        ColorSpaceName: "sRGB (float)",
        HasAlpha: false,
        Dpi: null));
}
@

\subsection{Color management}

The conversion uses WPF's [[ColorConvertedBitmap]], which runs the frame through the Windows color
system from the embedded profile to a destination context. A [[ColorContext]] made from a pixel format
alone, here [[Bgra32]], is the standard sRGB profile. Profiles found in the wild are sometimes
broken or of a kind the color system doesn't support; then the conversion throws, and the stored
values are used as they are, which means they are read as sRGB. A plain format conversion does that,
and is also the path for images without a profile. Both results are frozen: a frozen WPF object is
immutable and may be used from any thread, and this code runs on a background thread.

<<Win ImageDocument: converting to sRGB>>=
private static BitmapSource ToSrgbPbgra32(BitmapFrame frame, ColorContext? profile)
{
    if (profile is not null)
    {
        try
        {
            var converted = new ColorConvertedBitmap(frame, profile, new ColorContext(PixelFormats.Bgra32), PixelFormats.Pbgra32);
            converted.Freeze();
            return converted;
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Unsupported or broken profile: fall back to the stored values.
        }
    }
    var plain = new FormatConvertedBitmap(frame, PixelFormats.Pbgra32, null, 0);
    plain.Freeze();
    return plain;
}
@

WPF's pixel formats don't say whether they have alpha, so the formats with an alpha channel are
listed. The premultiplied and straight variants both count.

<<Win ImageDocument: detecting alpha>>=
private static bool HasAlphaChannel(PixelFormat f) =>
    f == PixelFormats.Bgra32 || f == PixelFormats.Pbgra32 || f == PixelFormats.Rgba64 || f == PixelFormats.Prgba64
    || f == PixelFormats.Rgba128Float || f == PixelFormats.Prgba128Float;
@

Core Graphics gives a color space a name, such as ``Display P3''. WPF has no such property, so the
port reads the name from the profile itself. An ICC profile starts with a 128-byte header, followed by
a tag count and a table of 12-byte entries, each a four-letter signature, an offset and a size, all
big-endian. The name is the tag with signature [[desc]]. Its contents come in two types. Version~2
profiles use the \emph{text description} type, also marked [[desc]]: an ASCII length at offset~8 and
the ASCII text at offset~12. Version~4 profiles use the \emph{multi-localized Unicode} type,
[[mluc]]: a list of records, one per language, the first of which gives the length and offset of a
UTF-16 big-endian string at offsets~20 and~24. The code takes the first language. Every read is
checked against the length of the data, and any failure leaves the generic name ``Embedded ICC
profile''; a name is not worth rejecting an image for.

<<Win ImageDocument: the ICC profile's name>>=
/// <summary>Reads the profile's description tag ('desc' in ICC v2, 'mluc' in v4).</summary>
private static string? ProfileDescription(ColorContext profile)
{
    try
    {
        using var s = profile.OpenProfileStream();
        using var ms = new MemoryStream();
        s.CopyTo(ms);
        var d = ms.ToArray();
        if (d.Length < 132) return null;
        uint count = BE32(d, 128);
        for (int i = 0; i < count && 132 + i * 12 + 12 <= d.Length; i++)
        {
            int t = 132 + i * 12;
            if (Encoding.ASCII.GetString(d, t, 4) != "desc") continue;
            int off = (int)BE32(d, t + 4), size = (int)BE32(d, t + 8);
            if (off + size > d.Length || size < 12) return null;
            string type = Encoding.ASCII.GetString(d, off, 4);
            if (type == "desc")
            {
                int len = (int)BE32(d, off + 8);
                return Encoding.ASCII.GetString(d, off + 12, Math.Max(0, Math.Min(len, size - 12))).TrimEnd('\0');
            }
            if (type == "mluc" && size >= 28)
            {
                int recLen = (int)BE32(d, off + 20), recOff = (int)BE32(d, off + 24);
                if (off + recOff + recLen <= d.Length)
                    return Encoding.BigEndianUnicode.GetString(d, off + recOff, recLen).TrimEnd('\0');
            }
            return null;
        }
    }
    catch (Exception)
    {
    }
    return null;
}

private static uint BE32(byte[] d, int i) => (uint)(d[i] << 24 | d[i + 1] << 16 | d[i + 2] << 8 | d[i + 3]);
@

The last piece renders a buffer for display, and every variant uses it, not only the original. It
plays the part of [[makeCGImage]]. The bitmap is set to 96 dots per inch, the value at which WPF
maps one pixel to one device-independent unit, so an image is drawn at its true pixel size on a
monitor at 100\% scale, and the views can compensate for other scales (Chapter~\ref{ch:win-views}).

<<Win ImageDocument: rendering a buffer>>=
public static class Bitmaps
{
    /// <summary>Renders a buffer as a frozen 8-bit premultiplied BGRA bitmap at 96 dpi (1 pixel = 1 DIP).</summary>
    public static BitmapSource FromBuffer(PixelBuffer buffer)
    {
        var bitmap = BitmapSource.Create(buffer.Width, buffer.Height, 96, 96, PixelFormats.Pbgra32, null,
            buffer.ToPremultipliedBgra8(), buffer.Width * 4);
        bitmap.Freeze();
        return bitmap;
    }
}
@

\section{Variants, zones and panes}

The small types of the session follow Chapter~\ref{ch:model} closely. The differences come from the
languages.

<<Windows/LineScope.App/Model/ImageVariant.cs>>=
using System.Windows.Media.Imaging;
using LineScope.Core;

namespace LineScope.App.Model;

<<Win ImageVariant: the variant type>>

<<Win ImageVariant: zones>>

<<Win ImageVariant: panes>>

<<Win ImageVariant: the frequency axis>>

<<Win ImageVariant: operation requests>>
@

A variant is immutable, as on macOS, and keeps its lineage. C\#~12's \emph{primary constructor}
lets the class take its values in its declaration. The Swift [[UUID]] is a [[Guid]] here, and
the arrow-joined name is built with [[string.Join]].

<<Win ImageVariant: the variant type>>=
/// <summary>The original image or one derived copy. Each copy records the chain of operations back to the original.</summary>
public sealed class ImageVariant(PixelBuffer buffer, BitmapSource bitmap, Guid? parentId, ImageOperation? operation,
    IReadOnlyList<ImageOperation> lineage)
{
    public Guid Id { get; } = Guid.NewGuid();
    public PixelBuffer Buffer { get; } = buffer;
    public BitmapSource Bitmap { get; } = bitmap;
    public Guid? ParentId { get; } = parentId;
    public ImageOperation? Operation { get; } = operation;
    /// <summary>Operations applied to the original, in order. Empty for the original.</summary>
    public IReadOnlyList<ImageOperation> Lineage { get; } = lineage;

    public bool IsOriginal => Operation is null;

    public string Name => Lineage.Count == 0 ? "Original" : string.Join(" → ", Lineage.Select(o => o.DisplayName));
}
@

A C\# enumeration is only a named integer: it can't have methods, and it doesn't list its cases. Each
enumeration therefore has a static companion class, with the list of cases in [[All]], the
counterpart of [[CaseIterable]], and extension methods that give it the Swift computed properties,
here [[Title]]. The zone's integer value indexes the pane arrays, as its raw value did.

<<Win ImageVariant: zones>>=
/// <summary>Which kind of content a zone shows.</summary>
public enum Zone { Images, Profile, Spectrum }

public static class ZoneInfo
{
    public static readonly Zone[] All = [Zone.Images, Zone.Profile, Zone.Spectrum];

    public static string Title(this Zone z) => z switch
    {
        Zone.Images => "Images",
        Zone.Profile => "Line Profile",
        _ => "Spectrum",
    };
}
@

A pane is a \emph{class}, where Swift had a struct. The Swift session changed a pane by assigning
into its array, and observation noticed the assignment. Here the session changes the pane's
[[VariantId]] in place and announces the change itself (below). The pane's own identifier is kept,
for the views to recognize a pane across rebuilds.

<<Win ImageVariant: panes>>=
/// <summary>One side-by-side pane inside a zone; it shows one variant at a time.</summary>
public sealed class PaneState(Guid variantId)
{
    public Guid Id { get; } = Guid.NewGuid();
    public Guid VariantId { get; set; } = variantId;
}
@

<<Win ImageVariant: the frequency axis>>=
public enum FrequencyAxis { CyclesPerPixel, CyclesPerLine }

public static class FrequencyAxisInfo
{
    public static readonly FrequencyAxis[] All = [FrequencyAxis.CyclesPerPixel, FrequencyAxis.CyclesPerLine];

    public static string DisplayName(this FrequencyAxis a) => a == FrequencyAxis.CyclesPerPixel ? "cycles/pixel" : "cycles/line";
}
@

A Swift enumeration with an associated value, [[resample(ResampleMethod)]], has no direct counterpart
in C\#. The usual substitute is a small closed hierarchy: an abstract record with one nested record per
case, which a [[switch]] expression takes apart by type, and a [[Resample]] case that carries its
method. The compiler can't prove that the switch covers every case, since another class could derive
from the record, hence the empty default. The request needs no identifier: SwiftUI's
[[sheet(item:)]] required one, but the Windows parameter dialog is simply shown, modally, by the menu
command (Chapter~\ref{ch:win-views}).

<<Win ImageVariant: operation requests>>=
/// <summary>Operations that need parameters before running; shown as a dialog.</summary>
public abstract record OperationRequest
{
    public sealed record GaussianBlur : OperationRequest;
    public sealed record BoxBlur : OperationRequest;
    public sealed record UnsharpMask : OperationRequest;
    public sealed record NoiseReduction : OperationRequest;
    public sealed record Resample(ResampleMethod Method) : OperationRequest;

    public string Title => this switch
    {
        GaussianBlur => "Gaussian Blur",
        BoxBlur => "Box Blur",
        UnsharpMask => "Sharpen (Unsharp Mask)",
        NoiseReduction => "Noise Reduction",
        Resample r => $"Resample: {r.Method.DisplayName()}",
        _ => "",
    };
}
@

\section{The session}

The session has the same state and the same methods as in Chapter~\ref{ch:model}, and the reasoning
there, from the normalized line to the shared analysis settings and chaining through the focused
variant, applies without change. What changes is how the views learn that something happened.

