A profile is simpler than on macOS. Swift Charts wanted a list of identifiable points; the port's
chart view (Chapter~\ref{ch:win-views}) draws from plain arrays, so a profile holds the positions along
the line, the channel values and the samples they came from.

<<Win Session: the profile record>>=
public sealed record Profile(double[] X, float[] Values, LineSamples Samples);
@

\subsection{Change notification}

On macOS the session was [[@Observable]], and SwiftUI tracked which properties each view read and
redrew exactly those views when one changed. WPF has a comparable mechanism, data binding with
[[INotifyPropertyChanged]], but it is designed for XAML bindings to individual properties, and the
views of this port are code that draws: an image canvas, charts, tab strips. For them a coarser and
more explicit scheme is simpler. The session raises one event, [[Changed]], after every change, and
the event says \emph{what kind} of state changed, as a set of flags. Each view subscribes and redraws
only if a flag it depends on is set. Dragging the line raises [[Line]]: the canvases redraw the line
and the charts recompute their profile and spectrum, but the tab strips, which show only the
variants, ignore it. Choosing a tab raises [[Panes]] and [[Focus]], and the charts ignore the focus.

The flags are powers of two, so a change can combine them with [[|]], and [[[Flags]]] tells .NET to
treat the enumeration as a set, for instance when printing it. [[Running]] covers the count of
background operations, which drives the progress indicator.

<<Win Session: change flags>>=
/// <summary>What changed in a <see cref="Session"/>, so views redraw only what they show.</summary>
[Flags]
public enum SessionChange
{
    None = 0,
    Line = 1,
    Settings = 2,
    Variants = 4,
    Panes = 8,
    Focus = 16,
    Running = 32,
    All = Line | Settings | Variants | Panes | Focus | Running,
}
@

Besides [[Changed]] there is an [[Error]] event. The Swift session stored an [[errorMessage]] for an
alert to observe; here the window subscribes and shows a message box. Both events are raised on the UI
thread only, as all of the session's state is touched only there. That was the meaning of
[[@MainActor]] in Swift; in C\# it is a convention that the code below keeps.

<<Win Session: identity, variants and events>>=
public string FileName { get; }
public SourceInfo SourceInfo { get; }

private readonly List<ImageVariant> _variants;
public IReadOnlyList<ImageVariant> Variants => _variants;

/// <summary>Raised on the UI thread after any state change.</summary>
public event Action<SessionChange>? Changed;
public event Action<string>? Error;
@

A new session has the original, focused, in one pane of each zone, as before.

<<Win Session: initialization>>=
public Session(ImageDocument document, string fileName)
{
    var original = new ImageVariant(document.Buffer, document.DisplayImage, null, null, []);
    FileName = fileName;
    SourceInfo = document.Info;
    _variants = [original];
    _focusedVariantId = original.Id;
    Panes = ZoneInfo.All.Select(_ => new List<PaneState> { new(original.Id) }).ToArray();
}
@

<<Win Session: raising a change>>=
private void Raise(SessionChange change) => Changed?.Invoke(change);
@

\subsection{State}

Every setting is a property with a private field, and its setter follows one pattern: if the value is
really new, store it and raise the flag. The comparison matters. A slider or a mouse drag assigns
the same value many times, and without the test each assignment would make every view redraw.

<<Win Session: the line>>=
private LineSegment _line = LineSegment.Initial;
public LineSegment Line
{
    get => _line;
    set { if (_line != value) { _line = value; Raise(SessionChange.Line); } }
}
@

The color model keeps the rule of Chapter~\ref{ch:model}: a channel index that would be out of range
for the new model is reset. The setter assigns the \emph{field} [[_channelIndex]], not the property,
so the two changes are announced by one event rather than two.

<<Win Session: color model and channel>>=
private ColorModel _colorModel = ColorModel.Rgba;
public ColorModel ColorModel
{
    get => _colorModel;
    set
    {
        if (_colorModel == value) return;
        _colorModel = value;
        if (_channelIndex >= value.Channels().Length) _channelIndex = 0;
        Raise(SessionChange.Settings);
    }
}

private int _channelIndex;
public int ChannelIndex
{
    get => _channelIndex;
    set { if (_channelIndex != value) { _channelIndex = value; Raise(SessionChange.Settings); } }
}
@

The other settings all raise [[Settings]]. Even [[UseDevicePixels]], which only affects the image
canvases, belongs here: it is a view setting shared by all panes, like the rest.

