The panes are an array of lists, indexed by zone, and the lists and panes are changed only by the
session's methods below. [[RunningOperations]] can be read by anyone but changed only by the session.
There is no [[pendingRequest]]: on macOS it drove a sheet from the session's state, while here the menu
command shows the parameter dialog directly.

<<Win Session: panes, focus and running operations>>=
/// <summary>Panes per zone, indexed by <see cref="Zone"/>.</summary>
public List<PaneState>[] Panes { get; }

private Guid _focusedVariantId;
/// <summary>The variant operations apply to and the inspector describes.</summary>
public Guid FocusedVariantId
{
    get => _focusedVariantId;
    set { if (_focusedVariantId != value) { _focusedVariantId = value; Raise(SessionChange.Focus); } }
}

public int RunningOperations { get; private set; }
@

<<Win Session: looking up variants>>=
public ImageVariant Original => _variants[0];
public ImageVariant FocusedVariant => Variant(FocusedVariantId) ?? Original;
public ImageVariant? Variant(Guid id) => _variants.FirstOrDefault(v => v.Id == id);
public string ChannelName => ColorModel.Channels()[ChannelIndex];
@

\subsection{Panes and variants}

The pane methods are those of Chapter~\ref{ch:model}, with the change events made explicit. Each
method changes what it needs and then raises one event that covers all of it. [[Select]], for instance,
assigns the focus through its field and raises [[Panes]] and [[Focus]] together, so the views update
once, after the state is consistent. Raising in the middle, with the pane changed and the focus not
yet, would let a view draw a state that never really existed.

<<Win Session: selecting and splitting panes>>=
public void Select(Guid variantId, Zone zone, int pane)
{
    Panes[(int)zone][pane].VariantId = variantId;
    _focusedVariantId = variantId;
    Raise(SessionChange.Panes | SessionChange.Focus);
}

public void Split(Zone zone)
{
    var shown = Panes[(int)zone].Select(p => p.VariantId).ToHashSet();
    var next = _variants.FirstOrDefault(v => !shown.Contains(v.Id)) ?? FocusedVariant;
    Panes[(int)zone].Add(new PaneState(next.Id));
    Raise(SessionChange.Panes);
}

public void Unsplit(Zone zone)
{
    var panes = Panes[(int)zone];
    if (panes.Count <= 1) return;
    panes.RemoveAt(panes.Count - 1);
    Raise(SessionChange.Panes);
}
@

<<Win Session: closing a variant>>=
public void CloseVariant(Guid id)
{
    int index = _variants.FindIndex(v => v.Id == id);
    if (index <= 0) return;
    _variants.RemoveAt(index);
    foreach (var zone in Panes)
        foreach (var pane in zone.Where(p => p.VariantId == id))
            pane.VariantId = Original.Id;
    if (_focusedVariantId == id) _focusedVariantId = Original.Id;
    Raise(SessionChange.Variants | SessionChange.Panes | SessionChange.Focus);
}
@

Performing an operation keeps the design of Chapter~\ref{ch:model}: capture the focused variant and
its buffer first, compute in the background, and append the result on the UI thread. The C\# tools are
different. [[Task.Run]] runs the operation on the thread pool, the counterpart of the detached task.
The [[await]] returns to the UI thread by itself, because WPF installs a \emph{synchronization context}
that sends continuations back to the dispatcher of the thread that awaited; this plays the role of the
main actor. The method is [[async void]], since a menu command starts it and nobody waits for it.

One step moves to the background: rendering the display bitmap. Swift made the [[CGImage]] on the main
actor; here [[Bitmaps.FromBuffer]] runs on the worker thread too, which is allowed because it freezes
the bitmap, and keeps the UI thread free of all per-pixel work. An exception, which the Swift
operations couldn't throw, is reported through [[Error]]. Whatever happens, the [[finally]] block
decrements the running count, so the progress indicator can't get stuck.

<<Win Session: performing an operation in the background>>=
/// <summary>Applies an operation to the focused variant in the background and adds the result as a new tab.</summary>
public async void Perform(ImageOperation operation)
{
    var source = FocusedVariant;
    var input = source.Buffer;
    RunningOperations++;
    Raise(SessionChange.Running);
    try
    {
        var (output, bitmap) = await Task.Run(() =>
        {
            var result = operation.Apply(input);
            return (result, Bitmaps.FromBuffer(result));
        });
        var variant = new ImageVariant(output, bitmap, source.Id, operation, [.. source.Lineage, operation]);
        _variants.Add(variant);
        Show(variant.Id);
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        Error?.Invoke($"Could not compute {operation.DisplayName}: {ex.Message}");
    }
    finally
    {
        RunningOperations--;
        Raise(SessionChange.Running);
    }
}
@

The new variant goes into the last pane of each zone, for the reason given in Chapter~\ref{ch:model}.
The index-from-end [[^1]] names that pane.

<<Win Session: showing a new variant>>=
/// <summary>
/// Shows a new variant in every zone: in the last pane when split (so the left pane keeps the
/// comparison baseline), otherwise in the only pane.
/// </summary>
private void Show(Guid id)
{
    foreach (var zone in Panes) zone[^1].VariantId = id;
    _focusedVariantId = id;
    Raise(SessionChange.Variants | SessionChange.Panes | SessionChange.Focus);
}
@

\subsection{Analysis and export}

The analysis is the same computation: sample the line in the variant's own grid, extract the channel,
and take the spectrum with the shared settings, with the decibel floor of $-100$. Only the return
types are simpler: arrays and a tuple of arrays, instead of lists of chart points.

<<Win Session: computing a profile>>=
public Profile Profile(ImageVariant variant)
{
    var samples = LineSampler.Sample(variant.Buffer, Line);
    var values = samples.Colors.Select(c => ColorConversion.Value(c, ColorModel, ChannelIndex)).ToArray();
    return new Profile(samples.Positions, values, samples);
}
@

<<Win Session: computing a spectrum>>=
public SpectrumResult Spectrum(Profile profile) =>
    SpectrumAnalyzer.Compute(profile.Values, Math.Max(profile.Samples.Spacing, 1e-9), Window, RemoveMean);

/// <summary>Frequencies on the chosen axis and magnitudes (in dB when enabled).</summary>
public (double[] Frequencies, double[] Magnitudes) SpectrumPoints(SpectrumResult s)
{
    var freqs = FrequencyAxis == FrequencyAxis.CyclesPerPixel ? s.CyclesPerPixel : s.CyclesPerLine;
    var mags = s.Amplitudes.Select(a => LogMagnitude ? Math.Max(20 * Math.Log10(Math.Max(a, 1e-12)), DbFloor) : a).ToArray();
    return (freqs, mags);
}

public const double DbFloor = -100.0;
@

Export writes the focused variant's display bitmap as a PNG, which is again exactly what is on
screen. Windows forbids more characters in file names than macOS does (among them [[\]], [[:]],
[[*]], [[?]] and [[|]]), so instead of replacing only the slash, every character that
[[Path.GetInvalidFileNameChars]] reports is replaced with a hyphen. The Save dialog is owned by the
document window, so it is modal to it.

<<Win Session: exporting the focused variant>>=
public void ExportFocused(Window? owner)
{
    var variant = FocusedVariant;
    string baseName = Path.GetFileNameWithoutExtension(FileName);
    string suffix = variant.IsOriginal ? "original" : variant.Name.Replace(" → ", "_");
    foreach (char c in Path.GetInvalidFileNameChars()) suffix = suffix.Replace(c, '-');
    var dialog = new SaveFileDialog
    {
        Filter = "PNG image|*.png",
        DefaultExt = ".png",
        FileName = $"{baseName}-{suffix}.png",
        Title = "Export Image",
    };
    if (dialog.ShowDialog(owner) != true) return;
    try
    {
        var encoder = new PngBitmapEncoder();
        encoder.Frames.Add(BitmapFrame.Create(variant.Bitmap));
        using var stream = File.Create(dialog.FileName);
        encoder.Save(stream);
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        Error?.Invoke($"Could not write {Path.GetFileName(dialog.FileName)}: {ex.Message}");
    }
}
@

\subsection{The active session}

On macOS the menus found their session through a focused value, with the active session as a fallback
for when the inspector was key. On Windows the first mechanism isn't needed: each document window has
its own menu bar, bound to its own session (Chapter~\ref{ch:win-views}), so there is no focused-value
entry to port. The inspector still needs the second. It is its own window, and while the user works in
it, it is the active one.

So the port keeps [[ActiveSession]]. Document windows set it when they are activated and clear it when
they close. A static class with its own [[Changed]] event replaces the observable singleton, and the
inspector subscribes to it, then to the [[Changed]] event of the session it names.

<<Win Session: the active session>>=
/// <summary>
/// The session of the most recently activated document window, for the inspector window (which is itself
/// the active window while being used).
/// </summary>
public static class ActiveSession
{
    private static Session? _session;

    public static event Action? Changed;

    public static Session? Current
    {
        get => _session;
        set { if (_session != value) { _session = value; Changed?.Invoke(); } }
    }
}
@
